window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][82] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> After 90 days, how will you know whether you are performing at the level expected of an associate product manager, and what if you are not?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;My manager will tell me.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names indicators and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines indicators with the manager at the start, checks them at 30, 60 and 90 days with partner feedback, compares with evidence (a shipped change, reliable updates), diagnoses gaps early and makes a plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd agree indicators with my manager at the start, in writing, so that the standard is shared: for example, that I can explain the product's users, metrics and decisions; that I have shipped one small change with a measure; that partners find me reliable and clear; and that my updates are on time. I'd check them myself at 30, 60 and 90 days against evidence, and ask for feedback at day 45 from my manager and two partners: one thing to keep and one to change. &quot;If I'm behind on one, I'd say so early and make a specific plan: if I haven't shipped, I'd shrink the work; if a partner finds me unreliable, I'd look at dates and scope promises I've made and fix the process. I'd also check my own calibration: if my read of how I'm doing differs from my manager's by more than a little, I'd ask for examples. The aim is to find out at day 45, not at the review.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Agreed indicators; evidence; early feedback; a plan; calibration.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"appendix-f\"></span>\n";
